  "query_preview": "..High pressure remains centered across New Mexico \nearly this morning but is expected to drift to the west through \nWednesday, elongating and becoming centered over Arizona. The high \nstarts to sink to the south and east as we head into the weekend. \n\nToday...The high remains centered to our east early this morning, \nbut it has started to slowly drift to the west. While the shift in \nthe high will eventually scour out some of the moisture over the \nregion, we should still have enough across our region to expect some \nisolated showers and thunderstorms to develop through this afternoon \nand evening. Activity looks to be largely confined to the higher \nterrain for today. \n\nWednesday through Friday...The high elongates and drifts to the \nwest, eventually centering over Arizona. The position of the high is \nnot favorable for storm activity and moisture begins to diminish \nacross the region. Best chances for any storms will remain across \nthe higher terrain from the Mogollon Rim through the White \nMountains. Overall, most areas will remain dry through the end of \nthe week. Expect hotter temperatures to return as the high \nstrengthens with maximum temperatures into the 100s in the valleys \nand into the 80s across the higher terrain. An Extreme Heat Warning \nremains in effect for the lower elevations of the Grand Canyon and \nYavapai County, for areas below 4000 feet, for Wednesday through \nFriday.\n\nSaturday through Monday...Models continue to show the high sliding \nto the south and east of the region by the weekend. This shift \nshould become more favorable for moisture advection into the region \nand as a result will bring increasing chances for showers and \nthunderstorms across the forecast area.",
